          "text": "It was Christmas day again and my two sons were 1 {{gap1}} at the prospect of unwrapping all the 2 {{gap2}} boxes that lay around the Christmas tree. I was 3 {{gap3}} about giving them their presents, but also a bit 4 {{gap4}} because I didn’t want to 5 {{gap5}} them again. There’s nothing more 6 {{gap6}} than a child with a 7 {{gap7}} present in their hands. That’s what had happened the year before. When my elder son opened his present I was 8 {{gap8}} to see how 9 {{gap9}} he was with his new toy. It was 10 {{gap10}}. But now it would be different, because I knew exactly what they really wanted. I had swallowed my pride and called my ex-wife to ask about what our children really wanted. When they were unwrapping the presents, I was concentrating on their expressions, and it was so 11 {{gap11}} to see the joy in their eyes! They were totally 12 {{gap12}} to see they were the new owners of an X-box with countless games. It was a 13 {{gap13}} scene. And I was so 14 {{gap14}} to see that this year I hadn’t failed them that I forgot about how much the idea of video games 15 {{gap15}} me.",
